  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Question": "On \\([0,+\\infty)\\), \\(y=x^2\\) is:",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "An increasing function",
    "B": "A decreasing function",
    "C": "Increasing first, then decreasing",
    "D": "Not monotonic"
  },
  "Correct Answer": "A",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "The vertex is at the origin. To its right, the larger \\(x\\) is, the larger its square is.\n\nAlternative method 1: Compare two arbitrary outputs\n\nTake any \\(0\\le x_1<x_2\\). Factoring the output difference makes its sign clear:\n\\[\nx_2^2-x_1^2=(x_2-x_1)(x_2+x_1)>0.\n\\]\nBoth factors are positive because \\(x_2>x_1\\ge0\\). Thus the larger input always has the larger output on \\([0,+\\infty)\\), so the function is increasing. The answer is A.",
